WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Speaker 1: Things are very rapidly escalating&nbsp;
in Russia. We've been waiting and wondering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:13.320
well, what is Putin's eventual move here? What was&nbsp;
expected by some to be an extremely rapid, total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:19.620
defeat of Ukraine, of course, did not happen.&nbsp;
And over the last couple of weeks, we have seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:26.460
Russian forces actually repelled and retreating.&nbsp;
And the question amid continued concerns about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:32.460
Vladimir Putin's health, has been what is the end&nbsp;
game? How is he going to ultimately get out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:38.040
this or is he ultimately going to get out of this?&nbsp;
The U.S. embassy is now warning Americans to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:45.480
out of of Russia. This is the clearest declaration&nbsp;
and the most comprehensive of you just have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.180
get out. Now, many of you might be saying, why are&nbsp;
there Americans still in Russia? There are so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.840
different reasons why there are Americans still&nbsp;
in Russia. And we're going to talk about that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:04.560
a moment. A CNN reporting the US embassy in Moscow&nbsp;
issued a security alert overnight that again urged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.840
U.S. citizens to leave Russia immediately. While&nbsp;
there are still options for departing the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.960
The alert comes in the wake of Russian President&nbsp;
Putin's order for partial mobilization of Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:23.460
men to fight in his war in Ukraine. Quote,&nbsp;
Russia may refuse to acknowledge dual nationals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:30.600
US citizenship, deny their access to US consular&nbsp;
assistance, prevent their departure from Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:39.060
and conscript dual nationals for military service.&nbsp;
That is a really scary statement which we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:45.300
talk about. The embassy has consistently warned&nbsp;
Americans not to travel to Russia and to depart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:51.060
the country immediately if there. The embassy&nbsp;
alert urged U.S. citizens to, quote, avoid all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.820
political or social protests. Do not photograph&nbsp;
security personnel at these events, noting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:04.500
the right to peaceful assembly and freedom of&nbsp;
expression are not guaranteed in Russia. Very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:11.940
different situation that Americans in Russia have&nbsp;
to understand. Leaving is a very good idea at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:20.100
point, but at the same time, there are people who&nbsp;
simply didn't research the situation properly, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.020
are there. Now, one of the things that's important&nbsp;
to understand about the dual citizenship thing. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:31.140
you are in a country in which you hold citizenship&nbsp;
and you hold an additional citizenship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.280
meaning you are an American citizen and you&nbsp;
are a Russian citizen, and you are in Russia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:44.040
It is often the case that in that country you are&nbsp;
only viewed through the lens of that citizenship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:52.320
If you were a French-American dual citizen and&nbsp;
you were in Russia. This is not an issue if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.740
are an American, Russian, dual citizen and you&nbsp;
were in Russia. The American citizenship isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:04.920
particularly relevant, at least not as far as&nbsp;
Russia cares. If you are a Russian-American dual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:10.200
citizen and you're in Russia and Russia says,&nbsp;
Hey, you know what? We are we are conscripting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:16.140
individuals to fight in the military. And you&nbsp;
go, Oh, no, I need help from the American embassy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:22.920
because I'm also an American citizen. It is very&nbsp;
common that that will be denied to you. Now, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.760
American authorities might say, well, we can still&nbsp;
try to help, but you will often be denied consular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:36.960
services because at the end of the day, you are a&nbsp;
Russian citizen. And this is something that I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.960
talked about and thought about in other terms with&nbsp;
regard to my Argentinean dual citizenship, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:49.920
there have been discussions of what if X, Y or&nbsp;
Z happened while in Argentina. This is obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.320
much more serious and much more significant. Now,&nbsp;
some reactions I've read are more or less like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:01.740
shouldn't these Americans have already left? I'm&nbsp;
actually I'm sympathetic to the people that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:07.500
there and that are also American citizens. It&nbsp;
is really difficult to uproot your life. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:14.940
are lots of people whose entire lives, despite&nbsp;
being American, Russian, dual citizens, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.640
entire lives are in Russia. They were born in the&nbsp;
U.S. to Russian parents and went to Russia at age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:26.460
three. Okay. That's that's an American citizen,&nbsp;
not nominally, whose entire life is arguably in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:30.660
Russia. And so the idea of, well, just get out,&nbsp;
what are you doing there? It's a more complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:36.720
statement than that might appear. The other thing&nbsp;
about this is that not everybody has the money or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.600
financial resources to get out and to come back to&nbsp;
the United States. It may not be obvious how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.420
would make a living in the United States. If you&nbsp;
are working in Russia and this is often something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.620
that comes up when we talk about undocumented&nbsp;
immigration to the United States and many on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:58.260
the right love to pretend that people that come to&nbsp;
the United States illegally kind of doing it on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.760
whim, kind of doing it for fun, for entertainment,&nbsp;
almost like some kind of tourism of some sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.640
And what's often missing from that conversation&nbsp;
is that these are people's lives. And uprooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.920
or moving your life to a different country is a&nbsp;
really, really big deal. And so even when you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:19.560
coming to the United States undocumented from&nbsp;
Central South America looking for work, as is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:26.280
this typical situation that we talk about, that's&nbsp;
a massive decision that no one makes lightly. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:32.100
it's often missing total lack of empathy when&nbsp;
we talk about and think about what it is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.600
these folks are doing here, not related to, hey,&nbsp;
Americans, it's time to get out of Russia. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.800
that also is going on. But these folks are not&nbsp;
on vacation is the important thing to understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:49.140
And this now gets us back to what is Putin&nbsp;
willing to do? How far is Putin willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:54.840
go? What is he potentially considering? And&nbsp;
is Putin sick? Is Putin erratic? Is Putin not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:00.300
thinking clearly? And the answers to all of these&nbsp;
questions may well be, yes. So there are some very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:08.340
non-trivial decisions to be made by Americans in&nbsp;
Russia. And the next 4 to 6 weeks of what Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:14.160
does or doesn't do could be extremely important in&nbsp;
terms of the entire course that this war is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:20.880
to take. Scary moment. And yes, all other caveats&nbsp;
aside, people have lives. People are all all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.660
things that we've talked about for people living&nbsp;
in Russia, the recommendation is to get out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:31.380
My preference would be not to end up potentially&nbsp;
conscripted into the Russian army. Now, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:35.280
I am not a Russian dual citizen and I have no&nbsp;
connections to Russia. So it's easy for me to say

